mcu_interrupt: do not scan pending sources when everything is masked

MCU_Interrupt_Handle() runs on every emulated instruction and ends by walking
every pending interrupt source, calling MCU_Interrupt_GetVL() on each, to find
one whose priority level outranks the CPU's interrupt mask.  Both are three-bit
values and the test is `mask < level`, so a mask of 7 cannot be outranked by
anything: the scan is guaranteed to find nothing and fall out of the bottom.
Returning early is an identity, not a heuristic.

Measured (callgrind Ir, mk2, 32 PCM slots, placeholder ROMs), on top of 0002:
7,761,848,566 -> 7,556,638,426, -2.64%.

READ THAT NUMBER WITH CARE.  It is almost entirely an artefact of the
placeholder ROMs: the fake firmware never lowers sr from its reset value of
0x700, so the mask is 7 for the whole run, and it never services the two timer
interrupts that are consequently stuck pending, so the scan it skips is 1.7
sources long every single instruction.  Real firmware lowers the mask early and
clears what it services.  What survives the caveat is only that the guard is
one instruction, is exact, and can never cost more than it saves.

Proof: patches/tests/mcu_interrupt_mask_guard_equivalence.cpp.

--- a/src/backend/mcu_interrupt.cpp
+++ b/src/backend/mcu_interrupt.cpp
@@ -215,6 +215,10 @@
         return;
     }
     uint32_t mask = (mcu.sr >> 8) & 7;
+    // Every level below is three bits, so nothing can outrank a mask of 7 and
+    // the scan below cannot dispatch. Skipping it is exact, not a heuristic.
+    if (mask >= 7)
+        return;
     for (MCU_Interrupt_Source source : mcu.interrupt_pending)
     {
         int32_t vector = -1;
